{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And embittered him and became adversaries.</b> His brothers embittered him, and Potiphar and his wife embittered him, to bind him — as in ״וַיְמָרְרוּ אֶת חַיֵּיהֶם״." "<b>And became adversaries.</b> His brothers became men of strife against him. And this form is not a pu'al, for if so it should have been pointed ״וָרָבוּ״, like ״הֵמָּה מֵי מְרִיבָה אֲשֶׁר רָבוּ״ (Numbers 20); and even if it meant רְבִיַּת חִצִּים, thus too it should have been pointed. Rather it is pu'al, like ״שֹׁמּוּ שָׁמַיִם״ (Jeremiah 2), which is לְשׁוֹן הוּשַׁמּוּ; and likewise ״רֹמּוּ מְעַט״, which is לְשׁוֹן הוּרְמוּ — except that הוּרְמוּ and הוּשַׁמּוּ are by the hand of others, while שֹׁמּוּ, רֹמּוּ, רֹבּוּ are of themselves — they make themselves desolate, they were exalted of themselves, they became men of strife; and likewise ״דֹּמּוּ יֹשְׁבֵי אִי״, like נִדְמוּ. And so Onkelos renders: וְנַקְמוֹהִי." "<b>Master of arrows.</b> Whose tongue was like an arrow. And its Targum is לְשׁוֹן וַתְּהִי הַמֶּחֱצָה — those who were worthy to divide a portion with him."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {1 "Rashi's grammatical argument turns on whether ורבו is pointed as pu'al (passive, 'were made') or another binyan; the verb forms he cites (שֹׁמּוּ, רֹמּוּ, דֹּמּוּ) are all lengthened pu'al forms that take reflexive force — 'of themselves' rather than done by others."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:19.503635871Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}